      "latency_ms": 2750.1,
      "prompt_tokens": 11,
      "completion_tokens": 788,
      "output": "At its most basic level, **water** is a chemical compound made of two elements: **hydrogen** and **oxygen**. Its chemical formula is **$H_2O$**—meaning every single molecule of water is made up of two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom. \n\nHowever, to humans, Earth, and all known life, water is much more than just a chemical formula. Here is a breakdown of what water is, why it matters, and how it behaves.\n\n---\n\n### 1. The Three States of Matter\nWater is unique because it is the only natural substance on Earth that exists abundantly in three distinct physical states depending on the temperature:\n*   **Liquid:** The standard form we drink, swim in, and use for rain (found in oceans, rivers, and lakes).\n*   **Solid (Ice):** Formed when water freezes at or below 0°C (32°F)—found in glaciers, snow, and ice cubes.\n*   **Gas (Vapor/Steam):** Formed when water boils or evaporates—found in the air as humidity, clouds, and steam.\n\n### 2. Unique Chemical Properties\nWater has several bizarre and unusual scientific properties that make life possible:\n*   **The \"Universal Solvent\":** Water can dissolve more substances than any other liquid. Because of its chemical structure, it easily bonds with salts, sugars, minerals, and gases, which is how our bodies absorb nutrients and how oceans stay salty.\n*   **Ice Floats:** Almost all liquids shrink and become denser when they freeze. Water is different; when it freezes into ice, it expands and becomes *less dense*. This is why ice floats on water. If ice sank, lakes and oceans would freeze from the bottom up, killing all aquatic life.\n*   **High Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb a lot of heat before its temperature changes significantly. This regulates the climate of the entire planet and keeps our bodies from overheating.\n*   **Cohesion and Adhesion:** Water molecules like to stick to each other (cohesion—which creates surface tension, allowing some bugs to walk on water) and to other things (adhesion—which is how water travels up the roots of giant trees).\n\n### 3. Water on Earth\n*   **The Blue Planet:** About **71% of Earth's surface** is covered in water, mostly in the oceans.\n*   **Scarcity of Fresh Water:** Despite all that water, **96.5% of it is saltwater** in the oceans. Only about **2.5% is freshwater**, and most of that is locked away in glaciers, ice caps, or buried deep underground. Less than 1% of Earth's water is easily accessible for human use in lakes and rivers.\n*   **The Water Cycle:** Water on Earth is constantly recycled through the **water cycle** (evaporation, condensation, precipitation, and collection). The water you drink today is the exact same water that dinosaurs drank millions of years ago!\n\n### 4. Water and Life\nWater is the essential ingredient for life as we know it. \n*   **Biology:** The human body is made up of about **60% water**. Our brains are roughly 73% water, our lungs about 83%, and even our bones are about 31% water. \n*   **Survival:** While a human can survive for weeks without food, they can generally only survive **three to four days** without water. \n*   **Astrobiology:** When scientists search for life on other planets or moons (like Mars or Jupiter’s moon Europa), their number one rule is to **\"follow the water,\"** because where there is liquid water, there is a chance for life."
